From: CipherScope <dev@cipherscope.io>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 10:17:54 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 27/32] test: cover timelocked tmr() leaves

tmr() parses its leaves in the context tr() uses for its own, so a leaf may
be any sane tapscript miniscript. A tree with an after() leaf and an older()
leaf exercises both kinds of timelock: which one the wallet can satisfy
follows from the nLockTime and the nSequence of the transaction being
signed, not from the tip, and with a final nSequence and no locktime neither
leaf can be satisfied at all.

The node then has the last word. The absolute spend is non-final until the
height its leaf names has been mined, and the relative one is
non-BIP68-final until its own input has the confirmations its leaf asks for,
each checked on the block before as well as on the block that lets it
through.
